# elohim/elohim-storage/src/trust — the trust-gradient seam (L5)

stage.rs + pricer.rs (PURE — the declared-stakes axis and the verification-depth
pricer) and snapshot.rs + memo.rs (stateful — signed head-set attestations and
verification memoization). Landed INERT: every caller of authorize_reach_for_human
passes TrustGradient::inert() and gets exactly today's behavior (NetworkStage::
Bootstrap, InertPricer always FullChain, no memo store). standing_projector (T19) is the
writer that eventually lights up live gradient behavior — until it lands, standing is
Unknown everywhere and nothing in this seam may claim otherwise.

This manifest is nested under elohim/elohim-storage/src/.epr-meta, which already
covers: subtree for the whole src/ tree — these four rules are additive edit-time
teaching on top of that coverage, not a separate coverage claim.

## The four rules

- **no-stored-score** (inject) — catches a `pub score:`-shaped field anywhere in this
  seam, the exact drift memo.rs's own module doc names as what its .epr-meta rail
  exists to catch. Standing is always re-derived, never cached as a bare number.
- **stage-cannot-cheapen-the-floor** (ask — the strongest class this manifest carries)
  — any edit touching a VerificationPricer implementor, price(), or FloorClass prompts a
  confirmation to re-run the keystone property test
  (every_in_crate_pricer_forces_full_chain_across_the_entire_product_space +
  conformance_harness_rejects_a_floor_cheapening_mutant) and to register any new
  implementor in tests::in_crate_pricers. A floor class other than None must force
  FullChain at every stage, including Simulacra — the stage most tempted to cheapen it.
- **dev-mode-is-not-network-stage** — NetworkStage must never derive from either of the
  two unrelated DEV_MODE/dev_mode flags already in this codebase (elohim-storage's inert
  one, doorway's live auth-permissive one). Absent/unknown declared stakes resolve to
  Bootstrap, fail-closed.
- **pure-half-stays-pure-{stage,pricer}** — stage.rs and pricer.rs stay free of
  diesel/tokio/a wall clock/an env read, so the eventual git mv to
  crates/trust-gradient (the module doc's named C13 successor) is a move, not a
  rewrite, and so the floor property test never needs a conductor to run.

## Why ask, not deny, even for the floor rule

Per the .epr-meta skill's class ladder, deny is reserved for genuinely-broken states (a
doc that cannot be linked into the substrate). Touching pricer.rs is not itself broken —
the 768-case property test is the real enforcement, and it runs at cargo test time
regardless of this rail. ask is the strongest class this manifest authors: one keystroke
lets a deliberate, tested change through; it never blocks a fix-in-progress.
